[2563] 2026-10-23T17:17:38 ASSISTANT: Two things happened, and both are the same kind of thing: the handoff was written from what I could see on this Mac, while the coding agent on the VM learned facts I could not see, and nobody carried them back into the handoff.

**The name setter.** When I rewrote section 5.1, I told the agent to drop the small forwarder it had added to OrcaSlicer's `Plater` and use OrcaSlicer's own public `set_project_filename` instead, because every line changed in an OrcaSlicer-owned file is one the fork has to re-resolve at each rebase. That advice came from reading the function's name and the verification report, which ran only on macOS. On Windows, the agent found that `set_project_filename` treats the dot in "Calibration · Gray PETG · Prusa MK4" as a path problem and empties the name. So it kept its forwarder, and you agreed with it in that session. The handoff never heard about that: it still says "no forwarder". The agent is right to flag it. The handoff should record the Windows finding and the one-line forwarder as a justified exception, with the rebase note that `fork-stewardship.md` asks for.

**"Starting from Home saves nothing."** I wrote that because, from the design's point of view, Home has no project open. The agent, reading the actual build, found that is not how the app works: when you go to Home, the last project usually stays loaded behind it. So pressing Start on a Home card is not "create a project from nothing"; it is "close the project still loaded behind Home and create another", and the agent chose to save a version of that project first, the same way the chip path does. That is the safer behaviour and the handoff should say it instead of "nothing is saved".

So: neither is a disagreement about the design. Both are places where the handoff stated a fact about the code that was wrong or incomplete, the agent checked the code, and the code won. That is the right order of authority, and the fix is to write both findings into the handoff so the next reader does not relearn them. I'll do that if you say so, or the original session can, since it holds the handoff: [the session this one was forked from](#local_55a98461-8604-47d5-87b0-2a7c9d1c964a).

